    "turn_text": "title essential health benefits which you know who can be against essential health benefits why don't they call them what they really are these are federally mandated insurance coverage is and Minnesota is the leader in this if they were giving out an award Minnesota the state of Minnesota would get the award for the most mandated coverage is because I think we're close to 70 mandated coverage is We don't need the federal government layering on top of that and giving us more federally mandated coverages. But the idea of this bill was take it away from the feds. We have a state regulated insurance system. Give it back to the states and let them determine what coverages, if any, should be mandated. And the argument was a lot of the moderates decided to vote no after the essential health benefit piece of Obamacare was removed, which Again it was 10 to 15 of them. So one thing that I noticed in terms of following the coverage of this thing as it was being deliberated is that as you would read these different articles that were purporting to give you a picture of how the process was going, it was very much a snapshot of particular moments. There was a certain day where Mark Meadows, the chair of the Freedom Caucus, on one hand was saying, we're really optimistic this thing's gonna be great. And then later that afternoon was saying, this is horrible, it's going nowhere. It's not going to get a phone. And so to",
