    "turn_text": "more complicated role. And I don't offer that. I don't articulate that as any form of excuse whatsoever for the positions that he has taken now or in the future. But just to provide you with the context, with the understanding of what it is, what the influences are, what the motivations are. And this is something that on a significantly smaller scale, I've had to deal with myself. I set on a city council. And making that transition from activist who says and thinks whatever he wants, without much thought beyond what I personally want and what I personally think is great, I now have to work with other people on a council where majorities, majority votes control the direction of municipal policy. And I also represent a constituency that may or may not agree with my ideological point of view. And let me give you a couple of examples. As a libertarian, I don't think zoning is a great idea. I would do away with it entirely. In my perfect world, if I were king, which is kind of a strange concept for a libertarian to ponder, But if I were in charge of the world, there would be no zoning laws. The market would take care of it. The market in a context of liberty where people's personal rights, individual rights, were protected. So if you're concerned about your neighbor doing something that's actually going to cause a tort, an objective harm to you, then there would be mechanisms in place in order to prevent that or to indemnify you for whatever loss you incurred as a result of your neighbor's action. But this idea that you ought to be able to dictate to your neighbor, down to something as insignificant as whether or not they can have one of those free little libraries in their front yard, right? Or how many feet their driveway has to be? you know, or how far their garage can be from the edge of their property line and what have you. The minute detail to which your personal property is controlled, that is something that is anathema to me and that I personally disagree with on principle. And yet, I have to recognize in my role as an elected official on a city council that I am the, well, to my knowledge, the only person who feels that way about it. I'm not getting anywhere with that line of thinking. Not only am I the only person who thinks that way on the council most likely, but I also live in a community, like many communities, I would hazard to guess all, for the most part, that generally like the idea of zoning. Because people generally like to have some sense of control, some sense of predictability regarding what's going to happen in their neighborhood and in their city. And I get that. I may not agree with it philosophically. I may not agree with it ideologically, but it's the way things are. And so I have to apply my principles in a context where there are political limitations to what can be accomplished. That general principle applies to every level of government and to every elected office held. Here's the key difference though. between myself in that municipal context and Republicans in Congress operating in Washington DC right now. They are in total control. They hold all the levers of power. Right now, they're trying to make it sound like, oh, we need to get 60 votes in the Senate, right? Like that's the last hurrah. That's the last hurdle we need to get ourselves over. And then we'll finally be able to do all these things that we've been promising that we'll be able to do. But that's a pretty ridiculous argument. They have control. They can more or less do whatever it is they want to do. The fact of the matter is, on the whole, as a caucus, as a party, they do not all want to do the same thing. And I think our interview with Emmer brought that to bear. What did you think of what Tom Emmer had to say to their bread? It was very candid. I thought he spoke, you know, just off the cuff like he was, I mean, obviously you two know each other, but it was, he didn't seem, you know, on guard. I think that regard