    "turn_idx": 109,
    "turn_text": "The thing I found most shocking in our interview with Congressman Tom Emmer earlier this hour here on Closing Argument on Twin Cities News Talk, AM1130, 1035 FM. was his surprisingly frank answer to the question regarding what Republicans and conservatives should make of the fact that Donald Trump is pivoting from the drama on the health care bill that didn't make it to the floor of the house to negotiating with Democrats. I'm not quite sure what I expected, Tom, to say. I didn't expect what he did say, which was essentially, yeah, you should be concerned. You should be concerned. When the president, after 17 days of trying to get legislation passed, tosses up his hands and says, yeah, I'm going to work with Democrats. Even though he has total control, he has majorities in the House and the Senate. You know, Republicans own everything. And after 17 days of trying, and less than three months actually in office, He's just decided that that's it. You know, it's enough, enough. He's got to negotiate with Democrats or to get anything done. What do you take from that? How, how are you? What does that mean? And the subtext, and I want to be careful here because this is not what Tom Emmer said, but the subtext that I inferred from what he said was that Trump isn't necessarily on the side of conservatives. Well, he said, do you think Donald Trump is actually a conservative? Right. That's pretty explicit. He ran on being blustery, not conservative principles. Yeah. Yeah. Emmer said that Trump was, that he believed that people were looking for, quote, the biggest, baddest something or another, unquote, to take on the left. And I believe, I think that's right. I think people were so sick and tired of one, the fake news, right? The media that's so biased and just in some cases overtly lies, but certainly is biased in terms of their story selection and the way that they present things. They got sick and tired of that. They got sick and tired of Barack Obama as president of the United States and his entire coalition of underlings whose mission in life was to try to convince us out all how horrible we are as Americans, you know, how America isn't offering anything of value to the rest of the world and how we have to be second, third, fourth or fifth instead of first. And here comes Donald Trump saying America first. Here comes Donald Trump taking it to him on Twitter fearlessly, right? And so he was a champion who was willing to take on the left. And people were so hungry for that. And they weren't getting it. And this is where Republicans take a lot of the blame. They weren't getting it from Republicans. So here comes Donald Trump, who was not a Republican, but took on the mantle in order to run for president. And so people got behind him because he was willing to take on the left. But my question for you now is, in retrospect, how does working with the Democrats take on the left? This is a question that we need to spend some time considering, because, for instance, I got a",
