    "turn_text": "just give you a little bit of a quote here. There is a reasonably realistic alternative path this author, who's clearly a lefty, writes, Democrats aren't going to accept Obamacare repeal, huge Medicaid cuts, or a system centered on private health savings accounts. Republicans won't go along with Medicare for all, or a government-run public option in the private insurance markets. And neither party seems likely to embrace a shift away from America's heavy dependence on tax preferred, employer-sponsored health coverage, even though many experts support it. But there are several constructive tweaks to Obamacare. Both parties could conceivably support. If you were even in the GOP repeal bill, if Trump decides he would rather be a savior than a saboteur. And just to kind of give you the rundown on what it is that this guy prescribes, he talks about defusing the crisis. That's his point number one. And what he means by that is basically conveying through statements that we're not going to move forward with repealing Obamacare. And the idea is to provide stability in the insurance market so that insurers actually feel comfortable continuing to provide services on the exchange. Because right now they don't know what's going to happen, right? Number two, and this is the big one. This is what I want to focus on is he talks about ensuring the insurers. And that's the idea that we're going to make the government the reinsurer for insurance companies. That was already built into Obamacare, I thought. It was already built into Obamacare, but he's talking about doubling down and making it basically official that you're guaranteed to be covered. And what the effect of this is, let me be as candid as I possibly can. And you're gonna scoff, but I'm being sincere. What they're talking about here is fascism. And I know that when that word gets tossed around, Godwin's law immediately gets evoked. And it's, oh God, here we are talking about Nazis again, right? By fascism, I don't mean concentration camps. I don't mean the Holocaust. I'm not referring to people being rounded up and put on trains. Fascism isn't any of those things. Fascism spawned those things, but that's not what fascism is. What fascism is? is it's public control, state control of private property. It's private property in name only. And what this author here at Politico is prescribing and what Trump is flirting with by negotiating with Democrats, what Democrats are willing to go along with, is fascism as applied to healthcare. Wherein, we basically will have a single payer system But it's going to take place under the guise of a competitive insurance market because all you'll be able to go and buy your insurance plan from insurers, right? And pay a premium and get coverage and have all the trappings of something that's yours that you're getting from them quotes around all that. But in reality, what it's going to be is the taxpayer backing it all up through this reinsurance scheme. So it's going to be single payer, just with a facade of a marketplace. That's what's being proposed. And you can bet that that's the kind of thing that Donald Trump is gonna produce. Because what it has the capacity to do is deliver on all of the promises he's been making. Everybody's insured, costs go down, your premiums go down, you get covered, quote unquote, right? All the things that Democrats have been promising, by the way, too. And it also delivers on a lot of the rhetoric that Republicans have been throwing around without, because they didn't want to take responsibility for what the free market actually requires. This is closing argument, 651-989-585-55. We'll be back in a moment, Twin Cities News Talk, AM11-30-1035-FM, and TwinCitiesNewsTalk.com.",
